# Durable index store, slice 3: fix round 1

All three sign-off findings are addressed and every lane passed. Nothing is staged or committed. The full report is at `collab/1790768800000-storage-engineer-repl-durable-index-slice3.claude-opus-5-5.report-r2.md`.

Only finding 3 was a real defect. The rehydration code already handled findings 1 and 2 correctly; the tests just didn't prove it, and now they do.

**New fixture.** It publishes three transactions through a real transactor into a durable directory and moves HEAD to them, then a shell starts on that directory. The transactions land at `t` 0, 1 and 2:
- two assertions;
- an assertion whose metadata id, 40, is the greatest id in the fixture and appears only in `m`;
- a retraction of the first fact.

**1. Several `t` values and a retraction.** `a-restart-restores-every-transaction-group-history-row-and-retraction` asserts the exact restored transaction groups, the full history view (retraction included), and a current view of exactly `#{[17 "b"] [18 "c"]}`. It checks the history and current views before and after a new publication.
- Each of three mutations fails it with 3 failures and 4 errors: regrouping all datoms into one transaction, rewriting an older `t`, or dropping retractions.
- The errors are `q` answering an `Error: …` refusal instead of a relation.
- My first draft had a test bug, not a code defect: it read the restored groups after later rounds had appended to the live log. It now captures them before any evaluation.

**2. The metadata-id rule.** The same test asserts every id allocated after the restart is above 40. If allocation ignores `m`, the test fails, because ids would resume at 19.

**3. The `(vm …)` message.** It now depends on the store mode:
- durable: `Switched to <VM> (VM store cleared; durable code index kept)`;
- memory: unchanged, `… (store cleared)`, so the existing assertions still hold.

`vm-selection-says-what-it-keeps` failed before the fix and passes after. Forcing the memory message in durable mode fails it.

